{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>For I lift up My hand to heaven.</b> This promise of future redemption is by oath, for everyone who swears lifts his hand and touches the object by which he swears. It is in the manner of what is written in Daniel regarding this promise of redemption (Daniel 12:7): ״וירם ימינו ושמאלו אל השמים וישבע בחי העולם וגו׳״, and so David said, of blessed memory (Psalms 10:12): ״קומה ה' אל נשא ידך״. And so we find regarding the promise of the redemption from Egypt, that it was by oath, for there it is written (Exodus 6:6): ״אשר נשאתי את ידי״, and it is written (ibid.): ״לכן אמור לבני ישראל אני ה'״ — and therefore it is the language of an oath, as in (I Samuel 3:14): ״לכן נשבעתי לבית עלי״. And similarly it is written regarding the future redemption (Isaiah 30:18): ״ולכן יחכה ה' לחננכם ולכן ירום לרחמכם״." "<b>And the Ramban wrote</b>, of blessed memory: כי אשא אל שמים ידי — by way of truth (emet), because during the time of exile He cast from heaven to earth the glory of Israel; He says He will now lift it up, in a time of favor, to the supernal heavens. And it is 'the great hand' that does battle for Israel. And this is the sense of ואמרתי חי אנכי לעולם — for I revive My strong hand when I flash the lightning of My sword and steady it to take hold of the judgment of Israel and return vengeance upon My foes. For then the Name will be whole and the Throne will be whole; and the discerning will understand — thus far his words, of blessed memory."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:33.615645150Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}